// Cerberus service worker: caches the app shell (HTML, JS, CSS, fonts,
// icons) so the UI opens when the host is unreachable, and nothing else.
//
// Never cached, never answered from cache: /api (including every event
// stream), /v1 (the gateway), /preview, /healthz, this file, any request
// that asks for text/event-stream, other origins and non-GET requests.
// Those go straight to the network as if there were no service worker.
//
// Routing (route() below, tested by web/test/pwa.test.mjs):
//   pass      the browser handles it (no respondWith)
//   navigate  "/" or "/login" without a query: network first (so the
//             server's login redirects still apply), the cached shell when
//             the host is unreachable (the page then shows its offline
//             banner); a query (?token= login) always goes out. When the
//             host answers with an index.html other than this worker's
//             (a new build is installing), this worker's copy is served,
//             so the page and its cached modules stay one build until
//             "Reload to update" switches both
//   shell     a file in SHELL: cache first, network when missing
//
// The cache name carries BUILD, a hash of the shell's files written by
// web/tools/build_sw.py (go test ./web checks it is current), so a new
// build installs a new cache. The new worker waits until the page asks it
// to take over ("Reload to update" posts {type: "skip-waiting"}), then the
// old caches are deleted.
//
// Served by the daemon at /sw.js (scope "/"). Service workers need a
// secure context: the Tailscale HTTPS listener or localhost, never plain
// HTTP on the LAN.

